``peat parse`` now parses disk images, live systems, and mounted drives, replacing ``peat pillage`` (`#49 <https://github.com/sandialabs/PEAT/issues/49>`_). Disk images (raw, VMDK, qcow2, VHD, VHDX, ISO, DMG, etc.) given to ``peat parse`` are mounted read-only on Linux, Windows, or macOS, the files PEAT's modules can parse on each volume are copied to the new ``parse_files/`` directory in the run directory, and the copies are parsed. New options: ``--copy-files`` (``PARSE_COPY_FILES``) also copies the files found in directories, ``--collect`` (``COLLECT_FILE_PATTERNS``) copies files PEAT doesn't have a module for without parsing them, ``--no-recursive`` (``PARSE_RECURSIVE``) only searches the top directory, and ``--password-file`` unlocks encrypted images and volumes. The parse summary lists the ``disk_images`` that were searched and the ``files_collected``. Searching a directory now skips Windows junctions, iCloud files that haven't been downloaded, and system directories in the root of a drive or mount, like pillage did, and never searches PEAT's own output directory or reads FIFOs and device files.
